.TH MU2E-IPMI-TOOL 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-ipmi-tool \- run an ipmitool command against Mu2e DAQ BMCs, from a gateway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-ipmi-tool
.RB [ \-n
.IR HOST ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR CLASS ]
.RB [ \-\-execute ]
.RB [ \-\-diagnose ]
.RB [ \-\-show\-command ]
.I ARG...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Issues one
.BR ipmitool (1)
command against a set of DAQ BMCs. It is the successor to
.I mu2edaq-operations/scripts/mu2e_ipmi.sh
and differs from it in three ways that matter during a recovery:
.IP \(bu 2
The BMC password comes from HashiCorp Vault and is delivered to the gateway on
standard input, so it appears in no process table on either host.
.BR ipmitool 's
.B \-E
form is used, never
.BR \-P .
.IP \(bu 2
Destructive verbs are refused for the protected hosts before the command is
even built.
.IP \(bu 2
State-changing verbs require
.BR \-\-execute ,
so a mistyped host name in a hurry reads a power state instead of changing one.
.PP
.B ipmitool
is run
.I on the gateway,
not locally: the IPMI subnets (192.168.157.0/24 at MC-2, 192.168.150.0/24 at
the teststand) are not routable from off site.
.SH WHAT THIS TOOL DOES NOT TEST
It reaches the gateway with the
.I ambient
Kerberos ticket only. Unlike a real run and unlike
.BR mu2e-ssh-probe (1),
it builds no credential chain \(em no service-identity fallbacks \(em and it
does not check whether the default credential cache holds a personal principal.
Two consequences, both of which have been mistaken for something else:
.IP \(bu 2
It can fail against a gateway a real run would open, because the run would
have fallen back to a service identity and this will not.
.IP \(bu 2
It can succeed while a real run fails, or the other way round, when the default
cache has been displaced by a service ticket \(em on macOS the usual residue of
an earlier run.
.PP
Treat a working
.B mu2e-ipmi-tool
as evidence about the
.I BMC
credentials, never as evidence that the recovery can log in. For that, use
.B mu2e-ssh-probe \-\-run true.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " HOST" "\fR, \fP" \-\-node " HOST"
Target node, by its lab name, short or fully qualified. Repeatable. With no
.B \-n
and no
.BR \-c ,
every node at the location that has a BMC is targeted.
.IP
Targets are then filtered to nodes that have a BMC \(em but if that filter would
empty the list, the unfiltered list is used instead. Naming
.I only
hosts with no
.I ipmi:
interface therefore does not stop the tool: it runs
.B ipmitool \-H None
on the gateway and reports the resulting failure. Only
.B \-\-diagnose
rejects such a host properly, with
.IR "has no BMC in the topology" .
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1)
.B \-n ipmi \-\-hostnames
lists the hosts that actually have one.
.TP
.BI \-c " CLASS" "\fR, \fP" \-\-class " CLASS"
Target every node of this host class. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-l " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-location " NAME"
Location whose gateway is used and whose nodes are targeted. Defaults to the
first configured location, not to all of them.
.TP
.BI \-\-gateway " HOST"
Force a particular gateway to run
.B ipmitool
on, rather than the first one that answers.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " NAME"
Override the BMC username from Vault for this invocation only. The upstream
.I mu2e_ipmi.sh
hard-codes
.BR MU2E ,
and the Vault secret returns the same, so this is a test hook rather than part
of a normal recovery: use it to check whether a BMC has been given a different
account.
.I ipmi.username
makes an override permanent.
.TP
.B \-\-diagnose
Find a working username and cipher suite against one BMC by trying the
plausible combinations, read-only
.RB ( "chassis power status" ),
stopping at the first that works. Point it at one BMC: it is a search, and
running it across a class multiplies the failed authentications. Capped at nine
attempts, because BMCs lock an account after repeated authentication failures.
Prints the configuration change to make.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-command
Print the exact
.B ipmitool
invocation and exit, for comparison against a known-working one. It carries no
password \(em ipmitool reads
.B IPMI_PASSWORD
from the environment.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Really issue a state-changing verb
.RB ( "chassis power on" / off / cycle / reset ).
Without it, the intended action is reported and nothing is sent.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt before a destructive verb.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the per-node results as JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including the gateway chosen and the invocation issued.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.SH SAFETY
.BR "chassis power off" ", " cycle " and " reset
are refused for every host listed under
.I protected:
in
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5)
\(em by default the gateways,
.I mu2e-mgr-01
and
.IR mu2e-dcs-01 .
.B \-\-execute
and
.B \-\-yes
do not override this. Powering any of them down would cut off access to the
cluster being recovered, or stop the NFS server the rest of it mounts from.
.SH EXAMPLES
Work out why a BMC refuses the session:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-\-diagnose \-n mu2e-trk-03 chassis power status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Power state of every tracker node:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-c tracker chassis power status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sensors on one node:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-n mu2e-trk-03 sdr elist
.EE
.RE
.PP
The BMC event log, which survives the outage the host did not:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-n mu2e-trk-03 sel list last 20
.EE
.RE
.PP
Actually switch a node on:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-n mu2e-trk-03 \-\-execute chassis power on
.EE
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSING A REFUSED SESSION
.B "Error: Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session"
is the same message for a wrong username, a wrong password, an unsupported
cipher suite and a chassis with no standby power, so the raw output does not
say what to change. This tool adds a plain-language cause and prints the
invocation it used.
.PP
The credentials are settled, and in a normal recovery none of what follows is
needed: the BMCs answer username
.B MU2E
at cipher suite
.BR 3 ,
which is the first combination tried, and the Vault secret returns that same
username.
.I ipmi.username
therefore stays unset, and setting it is not a step in bringing the cluster
back. The rest of this section is for the case where that stops being true.
.PP
The quickest route is to let the tool find it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ipmi-tool \-\-diagnose \-n mu2e-trk-03 chassis power status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Checked by hand, in order of likelihood:
.IP 1. 4
whether the chassis has standby power at all. A BMC that is not running answers
nothing, and
.B ipmitool
reports that as the message above. After an outage this is the expected case,
which is why it is
.I not
treated as a credential rejection.
.IP 2. 4
the username. IPMI usernames are
.IR "case sensitive" .
Compare what is being sent with
.BR \-\-show\-command ,
test an alternative with
.BR \-\-user ,
and set
.I ipmi.username
to make it permanent \(em that overrides Vault without touching the secret.
.IP 3. 4
the password, with
.BR mu2e-vault-ipmi (1);
.IP 4. 4
the cipher suite \(em some BMCs need
.I ipmi.cipher_suite: 17
rather than 3.
.PP
A rejected credential is neither retried nor repeated. Every BMC shares one
credential set, so the first rejection settles the matter for the whole run:
.BR mu2e-power-on (1)
stops issuing IPMI and reports a single diagnosis naming the refused username,
rather than walking the same wrong credential through all forty-five chassis
and locking the account on each. Only a RAKP failure or
.I "unauthorized name"
is read as a rejection; the RMCP+ message above deliberately is not, for the
reason in point 1. Set
.I ipmi.stop_on_auth_failure: false
to override.
.PP
.B \-\-diagnose
is capped at nine attempts for the same reason: each failure counts towards the
BMC's own account lockout, and a tool that walked every combination could lock
the account it is trying to test.
.PP
.I ipmi.tool_retries
and
.I ipmi.message_timeout
are unset by default on purpose. The invocation matches the working upstream
.I mu2e_ipmi.sh
apart from
.B \-E
in place of
.BR \-P :
.B ipmitool
retries of its own accord, and pinning it to a single attempt with
.B \-R 1
makes a BMC that needs a retry fail to establish a session at all. The
gateway-side wall-clock bound is set wide enough that it cannot cut those
retries short either.
.I ipmi.extra_args
is the place for anything else that has to be added.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded on every target.
.TP
.B 1
It failed, or was refused, on at least one target.
.TP
.B 2
No credentials, or no gateway to run from.
.TP
.B 3
The operator declined the confirmation prompt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-on (1),
.BR mu2e-vault-ipmi (1),
.BR mu2e-ssh-probe (1),
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5),
.BR ipmitool (1)
